WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:07.298
In 1723, at the age of 17, Benjamin Franklin walked into Philadelphia with one dollar in his

2
00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:14.175
pocket. Carrying three rolls of bread (his only food) and dressed in shabby, working-class clothes, he made

3
00:00:14.176 --> 00:00:20.173
a poor impression. No one would have expected this young fellow to become one of the most

4
00:00:20.174 --> 00:00:24.352
respected men, not just in Philadelphia, but in the great nation he helped found and in the rest

5
00:00:24.353 --> 00:00:30.835
of the world. His bold democratic spirit and numerous accomplishments made him one of the most revered

6
00:00:30.836 --> 00:00:34.784
figures in American history. In Philadelphia, Franklin took a job at a local printer. He had worked

7
00:00:34.785 --> 00:00:40.091
at his brother’s printing business in Boston since the age of 12 and was good at his

8
00:00:40.092 --> 00:00:48.051
job. Within five years, he had traveled to London to buy printing equipment, found a business partner,

9
00:00:48.052 --> 00:00:54.424
and started his own Philadelphia printing company. In 1729, at the young age of 23, he became

10
00:00:54.425 --> 00:01:00.699
the publisher of the Pennsylvania Gazette . Franklin had an unshakable belief in fairness, open discussion, and

11
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:07.700
the free exchange of ideas. At a time when most newspapers promoted one political or private point of

12
00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:14.127
view, Franklin ensured freedom of speech in his paper. If people had opposing viewpoints on an issue,

13
00:01:14.128 --> 00:01:20.015
for instance, the Gazette would publish articles from both sides of the debate. If readers disagreed with

14
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:26.958
something they read, they were encouraged to submit an article saying why they disagreed. This democratic stance

15
00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:32.552
helped the Pennsylvania Gazette become the most widely read newspaper in the American colonies. Franklin was the

16
00:01:32.553 --> 00:01:37.327
leading figure of an intellectual club called the Junto, which he founded at the age of 21.

17
00:01:37.328 --> 00:01:41.775
The club members met every Friday to discuss books they had read and essays they had written. The

18
00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:51.714
only qualification for attending was "a thirst for knowledge and an open mind." As a result, the

19
00:01:51.715 --> 00:01:56.601
club was diverse: shoemakers, poets, merchants, and others from a variety of backgrounds all met to talk

20
00:01:56.602 --> 00:02:00.986
about philosophy, science, and politics. Reading was an important activity of the Junto, but books were rare

21
00:02:00.987 --> 00:02:07.931
and expensive. At Franklin’s suggestion, the members pooled their money to buy books for a library. This

22
00:02:07.932 --> 00:02:10.859
was the birth of the Library Company of Philadelphia, which is now one of the most important

23
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:18.464
research libraries in the United States. Franklin was always active in his city’s public life; he organized

24
00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:22.573
Philadelphia’s first fire brigade, started a public program to clean the streets, and later helped establish a hospital.

25
00:02:22.574 --> 00:02:27.411
In addition to his many other pursuits, Franklin was a prolific writer. He wrote essays on the

26
00:02:27.412 --> 00:02:30.173
popular issues of the day as well as humorous and entertaining stories. His most popular work was

27
00:02:30.174 --> 00:02:38.518
written under a pseudonym. Poor Richard’s Almanac was an annual publication that gave information about the weather,

28
00:02:38.519 --> 00:02:41.463
the tides, the cycles of the moon, and the major events of the year. It was filled

29
00:02:41.464 --> 00:02:48.060
with practical advice and words of wisdom. Many of the sayings Franklin penned, such as "Early to

30
00:02:48.061 --> 00:02:55.203
bed and early to rise makes a man healthy and wealthy and wise," "A penny saved is

31
00:02:55.204 --> 00:02:57.703
a penny earned," and "Fish and visitors stink after three days," are commonly quoted to this day. Franklin

32
00:02:57.704 --> 00:03:03.266
enjoyed great success as a printer. In 1730, his company was named the official printer of the

33
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:08.924
then colony of Pennsylvania, and the popularity of the Pennsylvania Gazette and Poor Richard’s Almanac made the

34
00:03:08.925 --> 00:03:15.258
business profitable. At the age of 42, he retired, but Franklin’s idea of retirement was not, of

35
00:03:15.259 --> 00:03:17.647
course, to sit around and rest. He had always shown an interest in science. As a young

36
00:03:17.648 --> 00:03:23.147
man, he had demonstrated, for example, that dark material heats up faster than light material. After leaving

37
00:03:23.148 --> 00:03:27.076
the printing business, he devoted much of his time to science. He discovered the principle of cooling

38
00:03:27.077 --> 00:03:31.915
by evaporation and charted the flow of the Gulf Stream; he invented bifocal glasses, the Franklin stove, and

39
00:03:31.916 --> 00:03:38.514
a new musical instrument called the glass harmonica. Franklin is best known for his most famous discovery:

40
00:03:38.515 --> 00:03:43.348
that lightning is electricity. For years, he had followed developments in the new field of electricity and

41
00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:48.795
conducted his own experiments. To test his theory about lightning, he devised an experiment in which a

42
00:03:48.796 --> 00:03:55.716
kite on a wet string is flown in storm clouds. At the lower end of the string,

43
00:03:55.717 --> 00:04:00.938
a metal key becomes electrified by the charge conducted down the string. Other experiments led to his

44
00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:07.314
invention of the lightning rod, which prevents homes from burning when hit by lightning. Franklin’s electrical discoveries

45
00:04:07.315 --> 00:04:12.481
were widely published and discussed by scientists of the day. In 1753, in recognition of his work, he

46
00:04:12.482 --> 00:04:17.871
was elected to Britain’s Royal Society, an organization that promotes scientific research, becoming one of the few

47
00:04:17.872 --> 00:04:24.625
eighteenth-century Americans to receive that honor. For all his scientific successes, Franklin’s greatest contributions to history came

48
00:04:24.626 --> 00:04:30.742
in the political arena. In his early forties, he was selected as a councilman and became a

49
00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:36.589
justice of the peace for Philadelphia. In 1751, he was elected to the Pennsylvania Assembly, and two

50
00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:44.792
years later, was appointed joint postmaster-general of the colonies. The years leading up to the American War

51
00:04:44.793 --> 00:04:51.959
of Independence were a time of increasing conflict between the 13 American colonies and Great Britain, and in

52
00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.271
1757, Franklin was sent to London as an envoy. In England, he spoke against Britain’s unfair taxes

53
00:04:56.272 --> 00:05:02.611
on the colonies and became known as a respected statesman. Franklin was one of the first to

54
00:05:02.612 --> 00:05:07.500
propose a union of the colonies, and in 1776, as war with England broke out, he returned

55
00:05:07.501 --> 00:05:11.540
to America and helped draft the Declaration of Independence. When representatives from the 13 colonies met to

56
00:05:11.541 --> 00:05:19.214
decide the constitution that established the United States of America, Franklin was a key delegate. In fact,

57
00:05:19.215 --> 00:05:25.157
they met in the rooms of the Library Company of Philadelphia, started by Franklin years before. During

58
00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:30.038
the War of Independence, Franklin served his new country as a diplomat once again. He traveled to Paris,

59
00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:37.689
where he negotiated France’s support for the United States; this alliance played a decisive role in the

60
00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.919
United States’ victory. During his many years in England and Europe, Benjamin Franklin’s fame grew. He spoke

61
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.190
six languages, and met easily with the leading scientists, writers, and politicians of the day. Franklin’s belief

62
00:05:48.191 --> 00:05:52.174
in fairness and equality was demonstrated throughout his life. He spoke out for the fair treatment of

63
00:05:52.175 --> 00:05:57.577
Native Americans, and negotiated a treaty with the Iroquois nations. He wrote that women had the intellectual

64
00:05:57.578 --> 00:06:03.454
ability to be as successful as men, if only society would allow them. Although Franklin became very

65
00:06:03.455 --> 00:06:08.407
wealthy from his inventions, he never protected them with patents. The point of learning, he wrote, was not

66
00:06:08.408 --> 00:06:16.057
to amass wealth; rather, it was to "serve mankind, one’s country, friends, and family." Benjamin Franklin’s extraordinary

67
00:06:16.058 --> 00:06:23.881
life was one of service and accomplishment that touched the lives of people from every race, class,

68
00:06:23.882 --> 00:06:28.596
and religion. It is no surprise that many call Benjamin Franklin the greatest man of his time.

